"Scaling limit of Sinkhorn-rescaled Random Matrices via Stability of Static Schrödinger Bridges (https://arxiv.org/html/2604.23461v1)\nciteturn1024view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2604.23461v1\",\"lineno\":187}); Total lines: 1520\nL167: Two features of this bound are worth noting. First, the left-hand side is the square of the Hellinger distance, giving Hölder-$1/2$ regularity in the $L^{1}$ distance between the margins. Second, the constant depends only linearly on the cost, milder than the exponential dependence $e^{\\|\\kappa\\|_{\\infty}}$ in the reference stability (Theorem 【52†2.1】).\nL168: By combining Theorems 【52†2.1】 and 【53†2.2】 via the triangle inequality, we obtain total stability under simultaneous perturbation of both the kernel and the margins.\nL169: ###### Theorem 2.3 (Total stability of SSB).\nL170: \nL171: Make the same assumptions as in Theorems 【52†2.1】 and 【53†2.2】. Let $d\\mathcal{R}=e^{-\\kappa}d(\\mu_{0}\\otimes\\mu_{1})$ and $d\\mathcal{R}^{\\prime}=e^{-\\kappa^{\\prime}}d(\\mu_{0}^{\\prime}\\otimes\\mu_{1}^{\\prime})$. Further assume that\nL172: \nL173: (2.5)  |  | $\\displaystyle e^{-M}\\leq\\frac{d(\\mu_{0}\\otimes\\mu_{1})}{d(\\mu_{0}^{\\prime}\\otimes\\mu_{1}^{\\prime})}\\leq e^{M}\\hskip 10.00002pt\\mu\\otimes\\nu\\text{ a.s. }$  |\nL174: \nL175: for some $M\\geq 0$. Then\nL176: (2.6)  |  |  | $\\displaystyle d_{H}(\\pi^{\\mu_{0},\\mu_{1};\\mathcal{R}},\\pi^{\\mu_{0}^{\\prime},\\mu_{1}^{\\prime};\\mathcal{R}^{\\prime}})^{2}$  |\nL177: (2.7)  |  |  | $\\displaystyle\\hskip 20.00003pt\\leq 8(\\lVert\\kappa\\rVert_{\\infty}+M)\\left(\\lVert\\rho_{\\mathbf{r}}-\\rho_{\\mathbf{r}}^{\\prime}\\rVert_{L^{1}(\\mu)}+\\lVert\\rho_{\\mathbf{c}}-\\rho_{\\mathbf{c}}^{\\prime}\\rVert_{L^{1}(\\nu)}\\right)+4e^{3(\\lVert\\kappa\\rVert_{\\infty}+M)\\vee\\lVert(\\kappa^{\\prime})\\rVert_{\\infty}}\\,d_{H}(\\mathcal{R},\\mathcal{R}^{\\prime})^{2}.$  |\nL178: ###### Remark 2.4 (Role of the constant $M$).\nL179: The constant $M$ in Theorem 【54†2.3】 bounds the Radon–Nikodym derivative $d(\\mu_{0}\\otimes\\mu_{1})/d(\\mu_{0}^{\\prime}\\otimes\\mu_{1}^{\\prime})$ and appears solely to handle a change-of-reference step in the triangle inequality combining Theorems 【52†2.1】 and 【53†2.2】. In the scaling limit theory of Section 【10†2.4】, where $\\delta$-smoothness (see Def.\nL180: 【59†2.7】) makes marginal densities uniformly comparable, $M$ is absorbed into constants depending only on $\\delta$, and does not affect the asymptotic convergence rates.\nL181: This total stability estimate is the main tool in our scaling limit theory: it allows us to control the convergence of a sequence of discrete Schrödinger bridges (with converging margins and kernels) to the continuous SSB.\nL182: The kernel and margin stability results above (Theorems 【52†2.1】 and 【53†2.2】) control the coupling $\\pi$ in the Hellinger metric. While these results are sufficient for establishing scaling limits, the analysis of finite-dimensional Sinkhorn-rescaled random matrices requires a complementary result that controls the discrete Schrödinger potentials (the logarithms of the scaling factors) in the $L^{\\infty}$ norm, and that remains valid when the input matrix may have zero entries.\nL183: The following theorem provides such a stability bound. The closest precedent is [【60†28】, Lem. 9], which establishes an $\\ell^{\\infty}$ stability bound for the scaling factors under approximate scaling by extending the uniqueness argument of Sinkhorn and Knopp [【29†40】]; however, the constants there scale with the ratio of the maximum to the minimum entry of the input matrix, and therefore degenerate whenever any entry vanishes.\nL184: Our result instead relies on a row-alignment parameter that measures, in aggregate across columns, the inner-product overlap between pairs of rows. This condition admits matrices with many zero entries, and ensures that the Sinkhorn scaling map remains a well-conditioned local diffeomorphism, allowing the potentials to be controlled via a Banach fixed-point argument on its linearization at the origin.\nL185: This is the key technical ingredient for the concentration theory for rescaled random matrices stated in Section 【9†2.3】.\nL186: ###### Theorem 2.5 (Stability of discrete Schrödinger potentials).\nL187: Let $\\mathbf{A}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times n}$ be a matrix with non-negative entries, and let $\\mathbf{r}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m}_{>0}$ and $\\mathbf{c}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}_{>0}$ be target margin vectors with strictly positive entries such that $\\|\\mathbf{r}\\|_{1}=\\|\\mathbf{c}\\|_{1}=N$. Let $\\mathbf{r}(\\mathbf{A})=\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{1}_{n}$ and $\\mathbf{c}(\\mathbf{A})=\\mathbf{A}^{T}\\mathbf{1}_{m}$ denote the current row and column margins of $\\mathbf{A}$.\nL188: Assume that the target margin $(\\mathbf{r},\\mathbf{c})$ is close to the margin of $\\mathbf{A}$ in the sense that\nL189: (2.8)  |  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{r}(\\mathbf{A})\\in[(1-\\varepsilon)\\mathbf{r},(1+\\varepsilon)\\mathbf{r}],\\hskip 20.00003pt\\mathbf{c}(\\mathbf{A})\\in[(1-\\varepsilon)\\mathbf{c},(1+\\varepsilon)\\mathbf{c}]$  |\nL190: \nL191: entry-wise for some $\\epsilon\\in(0,\\epsilon_{\\textup{max}})$, where\nL192: (2.9)  |  | $\\displaystyle\\varepsilon_{\\max}:=\\frac{1}{50C_{\\mathbf{A}}^{2}},\\hskip 10.00002ptC_{\\mathbf{A}}:=1+\\frac{9\\max_{i}\\mathbf{r}_{i}\\max_{j}\\mathbf{c}_{j}}{\\rho_{\\mathbf{A}}mn},\\hskip 10.00002pt\\rho_{\\mathbf{A}}:=\\min_{i_{1},i_{2}\\in\\{1,\\dots,m\\}}n^{-1}\\sum_{j=1}^{n}\\mathbf{A}_{i_{1}j}\\mathbf{A}_{i_{2}j}.$  |\nL193: Then there exist exact Schrödinger potentials $\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}_{\\mathbf{A}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m},\\boldsymbol{\\beta}_{\\mathbf{A}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ such that $\\diag(e^{\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}_{\\mathbf{A}}})\\mathbf{A}\\diag(e^{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}_{\\mathbf{A}}})$ has exactly the margins $(\\mathbf{r},\\mathbf{c})$.\nL194: These potentials are unique up to the one-parameter gauge shift $(\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}_{\\mathbf{A}},\\boldsymbol{\\beta}_{\\mathbf{A}})\\mapsto(\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}_{\\mathbf{A}}-s\\mathbf{1}_{m},\\boldsymbol{\\beta}_{\\mathbf{A}}+s\\mathbf{1}_{n})$ with $s\\in\\mathbb{R}$, and can be chosen (via any canonical gauge-fixing, e.g. the one in the proof that projects onto the orthogonal complement of the gauge direction) such that\nL195: (2.10)  |  | $\\displaystyle\\|(\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}_{\\mathbf{A}},\\boldsymbol{\\beta}_{\\mathbf{A}})\\|_{\\infty}\\leq 4C_{\\mathbf{A}}\\epsilon.$  |\nL196: The bound (【61†2.10】) characterizes how the exact potentials deviate from zero when the current margins of $\\mathbf{A}$ are already close to the target margins. The term $4C_{\\mathbf{A}}\\epsilon$ reflects two distinct sources of stability: the linear sensitivity to the relative margin error $\\epsilon$, and the geometric stability of the scaling map captured by the constant $C_{\\mathbf{A}}$.\nL197: These stability results address different but complementary aspects of the perturbation theory. Theorems 【52†2.1】 and 【53†2.2】 provide the \"macroscopic\" stability needed for scaling limits, while Theorem 【55†2.5】 provides the \"microscopic\" $L^{\\infty}$ stability required for concentration analysis. Together, they provide a complete toolkit for analyzing Sinkhorn-rescaled random matrices.\nL198: ### 2.3. Concentration of Sinkhorn-rescaled random matrices\nL199: \nL200: We now turn to the finite-dimensional analysis of Sinkhorn-rescaled random matrices. The results in this section provide the probabilistic machinery needed to pass from the deterministic stability theory (Section 【8†2.2】) to the stochastic setting.\nL201: \nL202: Throughout this section, we fix a $\\delta$-smooth margin $(\\mathbf{r},\\mathbf{c})$ in the following sense.\nL203: ###### Definition 2.6 (Scalability).\nL204: \nL205: Fix an $m\\times n$ margin $(\\mathbf{r},\\mathbf{c})$. Let $\\mathcal{S}(\\mathbf{r},\\mathbf{c})$ denote the set of all $m\\times n$ nonnegative matrices $\\mathbf{A}$ that are scalable to margin $(\\mathbf{r},\\mathbf{c})$, that is, there exist positive diagonal matrices $\\mathbf{D}_{1},\\mathbf{D}_{2}$ such that $\\mathbf{D}_{1}\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{D}_{2}\\in\\mathcal{T}(\\mathbf{r},\\mathbf{c})$.\nL206: Next, we introduce the following notion of “smoothness” of a margin, first used by Barvinok in [【62†4】] in the study of random contingency tables.\nL207: ###### Definition 2.7 ($\\delta$-smooth margins [【62†4】]).\nL208: \nL209: An $m\\times n$ margin $(\\mathbf{r},\\mathbf{c})$ with total sum $N:=\\|\\mathbf{r}\\|_{1}=\\|\\mathbf{c}\\|_{1}$ is $\\delta$-smooth for some $\\delta>0$ if\nL210: \nL211: (2.11)  |  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\delta N}{m}\\leq\\mathbf{r}(i)\\leq\\frac{N}{\\delta m},\\hskip 20.00003pt\\frac{\\delta N}{n}\\leq\\mathbf{c}(j)\\leq\\frac{N}{\\delta n}$  |\nL212: \nL213: for all $i\\in[m]$ and $j\\in[n]$.\nL214: Note that if $(\\mathbf{r},\\mathbf{c})$ is a positive margin, then it is $\\delta$-smooth for some $\\delta=\\delta(\\mathbf{r},\\mathbf{c})>0$.\nL215: \nL216: The random matrices we consider satisfy the following standing assumptions.\nL217: ###### Assumption 2.8 (Subexponential random matrix).\nL218: \nL219: Let $\\mathbf{X}\\in\\mathbb{R}_{\\geq 0}^{m\\times n}$ be a random matrix with independent nonnegative entries and positive mean $\\mathbb{E}[\\mathbf{X}]=\\boldsymbol{\\Lambda}\\in\\mathbb{R}_{>0}^{m\\times n}$. There exist constants $\\sigma,R>0$ such that for all $q\\geq 2$,\nL220: \nL221: (2.12)  |  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}\\left[|\\mathbf{X}_{ij}-\\boldsymbol{\\Lambda}_{ij}|^{q}\\right]\\leq\\frac{q!}{2}\\sigma^{2}R^{q-2}\\hskip 20.00003pt\\textup{for all $i,j$}.$  |\nL222: ###### Assumption 2.9 (Bounded cost).\nL223: \nL224: Let $(\\mathbf{r},\\mathbf{c})$ be a $\\delta$-smooth margin and $\\boldsymbol{\\Lambda}\\in\\mathbb{R}_{>0}^{m\\times n}$. There exists a constant $K\\geq 0$ such that\nL225: \nL226: (2.13)  |  | $\\displaystyle\\left|\\log\\frac{\\mathbf{r}(i)\\mathbf{c}(j)\\,\\|\\boldsymbol{\\Lambda}\\|_{1}}{\\boldsymbol{\\Lambda}_{ij}N^{2}}\\right|\\leq K\\hskip 20.00003pt\\textup{for all $i\\in[m],j\\in[n]$}.$  |\nL227: Assumption 【63†2.8】 accommodates a wide class of random matrix ensembles, holding for Poisson, bounded, and general sub-exponential entry distributions. Assumption 【64†2.9】 states that the normalized mean matrix $\\boldsymbol{\\Lambda}/\\|\\boldsymbol{\\Lambda}\\|_{1}$ has bounded cost relative to the product of the normalized margins; it serves as the discrete analogue of the bounded-cost condition in Section 【8†2.2】.\nL228: ###### Remark 2.10 (Scalability is not assumed).\nL229: Note that scalability of $\\mathbf{X}$ to the target margin $(\\mathbf{r},\\mathbf{c})$ is not imposed as a hypothesis in Assumptions 【63†2.8】–【64†2.9】: Theorem 【45†2.11】 will show that $\\mathbf{X}\\in\\mathcal{S}(\\mathbf{r},\\mathbf{c})$ holds automatically on a high-probability event under these assumptions and $\\delta$-smoothness of the margin.\nL230: For ensembles outside the scope of Assumption 【63†2.8】, a matrix-ensemble-agnostic bound on the scalability probability—purely in terms of the smoothness parameter and the maximum zero-probability of entries—can be derived from the Menon–Schneider characterization [【56†33】]; we record this separately as Lemma 【65†5.2】 in Section 【22†5】.\nL231: We now apply the deterministic stability bound of Theorem 【55†2.5】 to the random matrix $\\mathbf{X}$ with target margin $(\\mathbf{r},\\mathbf{c})$ to obtain concentration of the random Schrödinger potentials around their deterministic counterparts. Specializing the random quantity $C_{\\mathbf{X}}$ from (【66†2.9】) (with $\\mathbf{A}=\\mathbf{X}$) requires controlling the margin sizes and the row-alignment parameter.\nL232: We use $\\delta$-smoothness of $(\\mathbf{r},\\mathbf{c})$ to deduce $\\max_{i}\\mathbf{r}(i)\\max_{j}\\mathbf{c}(j)\\leq N^{2}/(\\delta^{2}mn)$, while we obtain a high-probability lower bound on $\\rho_{\\mathbf{X}}$ in terms of $\\boldsymbol{\\Lambda}_{\\min}$ and $K$, where $K$ is the bound on the cost function in (【67†2.13】).\nL233: Substituting these estimates into (【66†2.9】) yields the explicit deterministic constant $C_{*}$ defined in (【68†2.14】) below, which serves as a high-probability upper bound for $C_{\\mathbf{X}}$.\nL234: Crucially, under Assumptions 【63†2.8】–【64†2.9】 and $\\delta$-smoothness the parameters $\\delta,K$, and $\\boldsymbol{\\Lambda}_{\\min}/\\boldsymbol{\\Lambda}_{\\max}$ are bounded away from zero, so $C_{*}=O(1)$; consequently the $4C_{\\mathbf{X}}\\varepsilon$ bound of Theorem 【55†2.5】 reduces to an order-$\\varepsilon$ control of the random potentials.\nL235: ###### Theorem 2.11 (Concentration of random potentials).\nL236: Let $(\\mathbf{r},\\mathbf{c})$ be an $m\\times n$ $\\delta$-smooth margin. Under Assumptions 【63†2.8】 and 【64†2.9】, define the minimum and maximum expected entries $\\boldsymbol{\\Lambda}_{\\min}:=\\min_{i,j}\\boldsymbol{\\Lambda}_{ij}$ and $\\boldsymbol{\\Lambda}_{\\max}:=\\max_{i,j}\\boldsymbol{\\Lambda}_{ij}$. Let $(\\boldsymbol{\\alpha},\\boldsymbol{\\beta})$ denote the deterministic Schrödinger potentials for $\\boldsymbol{\\Lambda}$ with margin $(\\mathbf{r},\\mathbf{c})$.\n"
